// Player 1 has unilateral control over reaching the target; player 2's single action is
// inert. <<1>> forces it outright in all three modes; [[1]] (dual: 1 plays adversarial,
// 2 -- who has no real control -- would need to be the achiever) fails in all three modes,
// at value 0 exactly (p1's "stay" is one fixed pure strategy, so almost/limit don't even
// get the usual approach-1-in-the-limit story: the value is flatly 0). <<2>>/[[2]] give the
// mirror image, and are included as a cross-check: [[1]] and <<2>> must agree, and [[2]]
// and <<1>> must agree, since each is the other's definition by construction.

// RESULT: true
<<p1>> sure [ F s=1 ]

// RESULT: true
<<p1>> almost [ F s=1 ]

// RESULT: true
<<p1>> limit [ F s=1 ]

// RESULT: false
[[p1]] sure [ F s=1 ]

// RESULT: false
[[p1]] almost [ F s=1 ]

// RESULT: false
[[p1]] limit [ F s=1 ]

// RESULT: false
<<p2>> sure [ F s=1 ]

// RESULT: false
<<p2>> almost [ F s=1 ]

// RESULT: false
<<p2>> limit [ F s=1 ]

// RESULT: true
[[p2]] sure [ F s=1 ]

// RESULT: true
[[p2]] almost [ F s=1 ]

// RESULT: true
[[p2]] limit [ F s=1 ]
